menuconfig KVISOR
	bool "kVisor kernel-based sandbox"
	depends on RUST
	depends on X86_64
	help
	  kVisor is a kernel-based sandbox for running untrusted code.
	  It provides a Linux-compatible syscall interface implemented
	  in Rust, running directly in the kernel for maximum performance.

	  Sandboxed processes see a standard Linux environment, but all
	  syscalls are mediated by kVisor's Rust implementation.

	  This is similar to gVisor but:
	  - Written in Rust (for memory safety)
	  - Implemented directly in the kernel (for performance)
	  - No VM overhead or syscall interception overhead

	  If unsure, say N.

if KVISOR

config KVISOR_DEBUG
	bool "kVisor debugging support"
	help
	  Enable additional debugging output and assertions in kVisor.
	  This adds overhead and should only be enabled for development.

	  If unsure, say N.

config KVISOR_MEMFS
	bool "kVisor in-memory filesystem"
	default y
	help
	  Enable the in-memory filesystem for sandboxed processes.
	  This provides a tmpfs-like filesystem isolated from the host.

	  If unsure, say Y.

endif # KVISOR
